# Environment for the spawned ACP agent being evaluated (NOT the judge keys).
#
# ── How this file is applied ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The agent is spawned with:  { HOME, USER, LOGNAME, TMPDIR }  +  <this file>  + TERM
# i.e. only four identity vars are inherited from the daemon — NOT PATH, NOT any
# API key. So this file effectively *replaces* the agent's environment beyond
# those four vars; it is not "a few extras on top of your shell".
#
# ── The footgun: missing vs empty vs partial ────────────────────────────────
#   • NO file (recommended for local runs): the eval falls back to the daemon's
#     full process.env — your shell's PATH and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY/etc. carry through.
#   • EMPTY file (or comments only): the agent gets ONLY {HOME,USER,LOGNAME,TMPDIR}.
#     No PATH → node/command resolution fails (the run errors before it starts);
#     no API key → provider auth fails. This is WORSE than having no file.
#   • PARTIAL file (e.g. just an API key): still no PATH → usually broken.
#
# So: only create `.env.agent` if you deliberately want to sandbox/override the
# agent's environment — and then it must be COMPLETE (include PATH + the key).
# Otherwise, delete it and `export` your keys in the shell before running.
#
# Note: USER + HOME always survive, so macOS-keychain auth (claude-code /
# claude-agent-acp, which keys its credential by $USER) works even without an
# API key here.
#
# ── If you do use this file, make it complete ───────────────────────────────
# Copy your real PATH so the agent can resolve node and other tools:
#   PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin   # paste your `echo $PATH`

# Anthropic-backed agents (or any agent calling the Anthropic API directly):
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# OpenAI-backed agents (codex / codex-acp):
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...

# Gemini (gemini --acp):
# GEMINI_API_KEY=...

# kiro-cli / cursor / custom: set whatever auth/env those runtimes require.
